Full Arabic RTL test (#127): defects that CSS and the Arabic overlay cannot change.
- The client's PostCSS converted every logical utility to left-to-right physical CSS (`.start-3{left:.75rem}`,
  `.text-start{text-align:left}`, `.me-2{margin-right:.5rem}`), so upstream components written for both directions
  were wrong in Arabic: the sign-in labels sat on the left until focused, then jumped right, and `text-start` meant
  left. Logical properties now stay logical (every supported browser has them); `:dir()` is still converted.
- Sliders (model parameters, speech) ran left to right in Arabic: Radix defaults to LTR without a direction. They
  follow the page direction now: the minimum is on the right.
- The floating label of the side-panel search boxes (memories, files, prompts, skills, bookmarks) sat on the left.
- /reset-password showed its form although password reset is off; like /forgot-password (0015) it returns to sign-in.
- The hidden heading of a user message read «نص جاهز 1:» (com_ui_prompt names a saved prompt in Arabic); it has its
  own key now (com_efaiw_message_prefix_user), English unchanged ("Prompt").
- Found on the way, not RTL: the phone drawer's close button handed its click event to setSidebarOpen as the
  after-slide callback, so every close threw a TypeError from requestAnimationFrame.

